From 533fc99b8b4168b0103b5cd4fbb5f1a27f842b35 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: James Reilly <jreilly1821@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 3 Sep 2026 03:57:49 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Carry to-device messages and real key counts through sliding
 sync
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The sliding-sync e2ee and to_device extensions were echo stubs: an empty
events array and a one-time key count hard-coded at 100, with the
long-poll deliberately ignoring both. For a client on sliding sync — which
is what Element X and the companion use — a Megolm room key therefore
never arrived, however complete the rest of the key transport was.

The device-key directory and the to-device inbox move into a shared
E2eeState with its own watch, reachable from the HTTP handlers and the
sync path without taking the application lock. Sliding sync now:

- drains the inbox into extensions.to_device.events when the client opted
  in, with next_batch carrying the pos token; the drained events are part
  of the cached response, so a retried request gets them again rather
  than losing them;
- reports the caller's real one-time key counts (the minimum across the
  user's devices, since the request names no device), and no unused
  fallback key types, since none are stored;
- wakes a waiting long-poll when a to-device message lands, via the new
  watch, and reports has-data for it.

A client that did not opt in is not drained. Legacy /sync keeps its own
merge on the same inbox.

Tests: real counts after upload, delivery and drain, no drain when the
extension is off, and a long-poll woken by a room key pushed mid-wait.
Proven end to end from outside the tree: two nodes on loopback, a client
session on each, an encrypted message decrypting on the far side.
